{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>“Unto Moses, and unto Aaron, and unto Miriam”</b> — Behold, Moses was not with them, but the prophecy came to all three of them at once. And the meaning of <b>“suddenly” (פִּתְאֹם)</b> — that at that time they were not directing their minds and preparing themselves for prophecy, and for the honor of Moses it came to them without any prior appointment for the matter, for “פִּתְאוֹם” according to the opinion of the commentators is a thing that did not arise upon the heart, from the root פֶּתִי. And Scripture says this for the sake of Aaron and Miriam, for Moses our teacher was worthy of prophecy at all times, and his mind was constantly set to cleave to the honored Name at every hour, as our Rabbis explained (Shabbat 87a) regarding the reason for his separation from his wife. But Onkelos (Onkelos on Numbers 12:4) rendered “בִּתְכֵיף” (immediately), and the meaning is that when they were speaking against Moses, while the matter was still in their mouths, it was said to them: “Go out, you three” — it was not delayed from them at all. And “פִּתְאוֹם” denotes swiftness, and so “I was suddenly curse his habitation” (Job 5:3), “which suddenly, in an instant” (Isaiah 30:13), “suddenly, without warning” (Numbers 6:9) — these are duplicated expressions for intensification, like “almost too little” (Ezekiel 16:47), “exceedingly much” (Genesis 15:1), and likewise “very, very much” (ibid. 17:2), and similar cases. And “פְתָאִים” are those who are most hasty, who have no deliberation in the matter and no counsel at all, as in “and the counsel of the cunning is carried headlong” (Job 5:13), and likewise “suddenly shall he be broken” (Proverbs 6:15), “and if suddenly, without enmity” (Numbers 35:22) — “בִּתְכֵיף,” and it is like פֶּתִי from the root פִּתְאוֹם. And the meaning of <b>“Go out, you three; and He called Aaron and Miriam”</b> — for He desired that Moses be present there and see the zeal of HaShem for his honor, and that he be available to them so that HaShem would not forgive them except through him, when they would plead with him and he would be appeased toward them. <b>“And He called Aaron and Miriam”</b> — He declares his praise not in his presence:"], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {0 "The heading citation from Numbers 12:4 is split: the lemma 'אֶל מֹשֶׁה וְאֶל אַהֲרֹן וְאֶל מִרְיָם' opens the comment, while 'צְאוּ שְׁלָשְׁתְּכֶם וַיִּקְרָא אַהֲרֹן וּמִרְיָם' appears later as a sub-lemma within the same comment."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:04:43.829537320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}